  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How to Fix Excess 401(k) Contributions</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> If your combined 2027 elective deferrals across all employers exceed <strong>$25,500</strong> ($34,000 if 50–59/64+, or $37,250 if 60–63), contact one of your plan administrators to request a <strong>corrective distribution</strong> of the excess plus earnings before <strong>April 15, 2028</strong>.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-back">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/" style="text-decoration: none;">Check If You Are Over the Limit →</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Common Ways People Over-Contribute</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Changing jobs midyear:</strong> Your new employer’s payroll does not know what you contributed at your old job (<a href="/401k-limit-changing-jobs/">see midyear job-change guide</a>).</li>
      <li><strong>27-paycheck calendar surprises:</strong> Using $980.77 × 26 ($25,500.02) or keeping a 26-paycheck amount during a <a href="/27-paychecks-401k-2027/">27-paycheck year</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Step-by-Step Fix Before April 15</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Verify Box 12 totals:</strong> Add Code D (pre-tax) and Code AA (Roth) from all 2027 W-2s.</li>
      <li><strong>Notify your plan administrator early:</strong> Request an “excess deferral corrective distribution” (most custodians set internal deadlines in March to process before April 15).</li>
      <li><strong>Report on your return:</strong> The returned excess is taxable in 2027, and any attributable earnings are taxable in the year distributed (reported on Form 1099-R).</li>
      <li><strong>Do not miss April 15:</strong> Excess deferrals left in the plan past April 15 are taxed <em>twice</em>—once in 2027 and again when withdrawn in retirement.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

